2026-11-13T14:24:22.911	{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GYBD3XDBDUyNbP8PXqEyJZ","type":"tool_result","content":"432\t#### 8.0-8 Manager Round Routine and Control\n433\t\n434\tThe standing routine every round brief points to; briefs list only the round's tasks.\n435\t\n436\t**Two round modes** (`fleet.toml` `[manager] mode`; default `resident`). `resident`: one Manager session lives for\n437\tthe whole round and does everything below. `calls`: the Manager is started as short separate sessions - ① plan\n438\t(Start + graph planning), ② judge (graph checkpoints, follow-up graphs; repeats), ③ close (Close) - and **the\n439\tengine runs `fleet_graph.py run` between them**; each call ends its report with a `NEXT: RUN G-… | CLOSE | STOP\n440\t<reason>` line, which is all the engine reads. The routine is the same; only who waits differs.\n441\t\n442\t**Round content, projects, records.** A round is launched from the web (`라운드 실행`). Without a round-specific\n443\tbrief (`.aimem/briefs/manager-round<N>-<date>.md`) the standard brief `.aimem/briefs/manager-round-standard.md` is\n444\tused and **the round's tasks are the `NEW` requests**; with nothing to do the Manager reports and ends.\n445\t- **A project spans rounds.** Each request names a project (`project` in the request file; empty = the fleet's own\n446\t  code, changed in place). Deliverables accumulate in **`projects/<project>/`** across rounds - one place, edited\n447\t  in place, no per-round copies. `projects/<project>/PROGRESS.md` is the project's memory: the Manager reads it at\n448\t  round start and prepends `## R<N>` (done, changed files, verification, **what remains**) at round close. Its\n449\t  top section `## 목표` is the project's goal checklist (`- [ ]` / `- [x]`): the Manager predicts the goals needed\n450\t  to finish the project on its first round and keeps the checks true every round - the web progress is\n451\t  done/total of that list (request average until the list exists). A new\n452\t  project gets a `README.md`. Assign paths to a slot with `aimem_db.py ownership set` before a worker writes\n453\t- **A round folder holds records only:** `rounds/R<N>/brief.md` (the brief as sent), `round.json` (launch time,\n454\t  open requests and their projects - written by the launcher; `task set --request` / `request set` during the\n455\t  round add theirs) and `REPORT.md` (the close report, also in the\n456\t  response body). No deliverables there; other rounds' folders are not touched\n457\t- **Identifiers stay as they are** (`8.0-3-1`); project and round are not prefixed to them. The web Fleet tab\n458\t  shows per-project progress: goals done/total (or request average), request counts, rounds worked (`round.json` + `PROGRESS.md`),\n459\t  and the latest `PROGRESS.md` entry\n460\t\n461\t**Start** (a new session is blank)\n462\t1. `fleet_ctl.py status` · `fleet.py status` · `aimem_db.py queue list` · `request list` · `slot list` ·\n463\t   `fleet.py runs --last 10` · `aimem_db.py handoff list --last 2`, then this header, `8.0`, `Section 9`. Trim\n464\t   output with `| head` / `| tail`\n465\t2. **Roster:** slots with role `(미배정 - 관리자가 다음 라운드에 정함)` or `events(kind=slot)` → set role and remark\n466\t   with `aimem_db.py slot set --slot S<n> --role \"…\" --note \"…\"`. Harness and model are not the Manager's to change\n467\t3. **NEW requests:** within scope and caps → split into subtasks → `PLANNED` → graph. **Caps are enforced by\n468\t   `fleet_graph.py new`:** new work stops at 3 graphs and 10 tasks per round; a graph that fixes review findings or\n469\t   re-reviews those fixes passes with `--followup \"<what it follows>\"` up to 6 graphs and 18 tasks. Never skip the\n470\t   re-review to stay under the cap. Otherwise\n471\t   `PLANNED` + \"next round\", mentioned in report item (5). A notification request from `슬롯 관리(웹)` is confirm →\n472\t   assign role → `DONE`. Mid-round arrivals are judged the same way at every checkpoint\n473\t\n474\t**Graph and verify rules** (standing; the round brief repeats them in Korean)\n475\t- **Free slots build and fix; paid slots (S1, S4) judge, design and review adversarially.** A build or fix given\n476\t  to a paid slot is listed with its reason in report item (6). Never move a failed free task to a paid slot\n477\t- **Small nodes:** one new file, about 200 lines, about 10 checks. Split larger work; split a file two slots\n478\t  would both write. Do not tell a worker to \"plan first\" - have it edit files right away\n479\t- **The engine runs `verify` confined** (bubblewrap): writable only in the repo and `/tmp`, `.git` read-only,\n480\t  `.env` and `.aimem/tls/` unreadable, no API keys, tokens or session markers in the environment, its own pid\n481\t  namespace; the network stays. A `verify` that writes elsewhere, reads keys or signals processes fails. Without\n482\t  bubblewrap the graph log says `검증 명령이 울타리 없이 돕니다`\n483\t- **When the Manager ends** (or a calls-mode call fails) the engine halts that round's workers that have no runner\n484\t  - node back to `todo`, attempt in `stopped_attempts`, not counted - before writing the minimal close. Workers of\n485\t  other rounds and hand-started ones are untouched; nothing is halted while paused\n486\t- **`verify` asks whether the worker did the work,** by exit code, never by grepping piped output\n487\t  (`… > /tmp/x.txt 2>&1 && …`). A node that writes checks is verified by \"it ran and the number of checks grew\",\n488\t  not \"the product passed\"; a product defect is reported `NEEDS-FIX: …` and fixed by a later node whose `verify`\n489\t  is \"all pass\". Run the `verify` command on the current tree once before using it\n490\t- **The engine fails an attempt that did nothing:** a report ending in a textual tool call (`aborted`) or a first\n491\t  attempt that touched none of its `write` paths (`no-op`)\n492\t- **Screen checks ask what is rendered** (`is_visible`, computed style, bounding boxes), not attributes; a screen\n493\t  behind login gets a check with auth on. Projects with a screen check overlap and overflow after input\n494\t- **Review copies of the repo exclude `.aimem/tls/`** and are removed by the reviewer when done\n495\t\n496\t**Graph checkpoints** (after each graph, so a cut session can resume from the DB)\n497\t- (a) `aimem_db.py task set --id T-xxx --status REVIEW|DONE` to match results\n498\t- (b) one `aimem_db.py event` line: \"G-xxx finished: per-node results, what remains\"\n499\t- (c) queue update (`queue drop`/`add`)\n500\t- (d) **close `REVIEW`**: reviewed task → DONE when the cross-review node is DONE; a request → DONE only after all\n501\t  its subtasks (closed requests take progress and ETA from the request status)\n502\t- (e) re-examine newly arrived `NEW` requests by start step 3\n503\t\n504\t**Close**\n505\t1. Full tests and linter; `aimem_db.py handoff add --bump --expect-rev N --body \"**REV N+1. …**\"` (≤1,200 chars);\n506\t   `render`; new rules registered in `8.0`. **`--expect-rev` is always given** - N is the REV this session last\n507\t   saw. A refusal means another session wrote meanwhile: re-read `handoff list --last 2` and the header, then\n508\t   decide; never retry with the new number blindly\n509\t2. `python3 tools/aimem_db.py archive` once (counts in the report), then `aimem_db.py migrate && render`\n510\t3. Report: `Status: DONE|PARTIAL|BLOCKED` + (1) task table (2) reproduced verification (3) waiting time (4) queue\n511\t   progress (5) next round's plan (6) operating metrics - slot utilization, Manager-only intervals, retries\n512\t   (7) one improvement proposal. These two are a standing duty. **No commit or push** (Claude Code does it)\n513\t\n514\t**Request lifecycle:** `NEW` (web/phone form) → `PLANNED` (Manager splits) → `IN-PROGRESS` (graph) →\n515\t`DONE`/`REJECTED` (Manager). Taeseok can edit title and body only while `NEW`.\n516\t\n517\t**Control table** - who changes what\n518\t\n519\t| Target | Changed 